<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-review-problems-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Review Problems #3</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These are problems on quadric surfaces and on the Chapter 4 topics that were
not assigned in Assignments 7, 8 and 9, collected here for review. They are
not assigned, but they are fair game on an exam. If you would like to review
the material first, see
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-quadric-surfaces" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-functions" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-limits" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-partial-derivatives" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-chain-rule" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-directional-derivatives" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-tangent-planes" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ===== Quadric surfaces, moved from Review Problems #2. ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rs19-3">
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface <m>4x^2 - y^2 + 2z^2 + 4 = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Dividing by <m>-4</m>, we can rewrite the equation as
<md>
<mrow>
\amp x^2 - \frac{y^2}{4} + \frac{z^2}{2} = -1, \quad\text{i.e.}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \frac{y^2}{4} = 1 + x^2 + \frac{z^2}{2},
</mrow>
</md>
which forces <m>y^2 \ge 4</m>, i.e. <m>y \ge 2</m> or
<m>y \le -2</m>. Let us look at the traces:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
Intersection with the plane <m>x = c_1</m>:
<m>\dfrac{y^2}{4} - \dfrac{z^2}{2} = 1 + c_1^2</m>, a hyperbola.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Intersection with the plane <m>y = c_2</m>:
<m>x^2 + \dfrac{z^2}{2} = \dfrac{c_2^2}{4} - 1</m>; when
<m>|c_2| \gt 2</m>, we have ellipses as intersections.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Intersection with the plane <m>z = c_3</m>:
<m>\dfrac{y^2}{4} - x^2 = 1 + \dfrac{c_3^2}{2}</m>, a hyperbola.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
The surface is a <term>hyperboloid of two sheets</term> along the
<m>y</m>-axis, with vertices <m>(0,-2,0)</m> and <m>(0,2,0)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rs19-hyperboloid2"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rs19-hyperboloid2">
<caption>
The hyperboloid of two sheets
<m>\frac{y^2}{4} - x^2 - \frac{z^2}{2} = 1</m>: two bowl-shaped
sheets opening along the <m>y</m>-axis, with vertices at
<m>(0,\pm 2,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Two bowl shaped surfaces in a three dimensional coordinate system,
opening away from each other along the horizontal y axis. The left
sheet has its vertex at zero comma negative two comma zero and opens
to the left; the right sheet has its vertex at zero comma two comma
zero and opens to the right. Each sheet ends in an ellipse.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-2.3) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-3.9,0) -- (4.1,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.9,0.65) -- (-1.25,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
  % right sheet: vertex at y=2, profile z = +-0.95*sqrt(y^2/2 - 2)
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.5, samples=31]
    (\x, {0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=2:3.5, samples=31]
    (\x, {-0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] (3.5,0) ellipse (0.34cm and 1.9cm);
  % left sheet
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=-3.5:-2, samples=31]
    (\x, {0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=-3.5:-2, samples=31]
    (\x, {-0.95*sqrt(0.5*\x*\x - 2)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] (-3.5,0) ellipse (0.34cm and 1.9cm);
  % vertices
  \fill (2,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=1pt] {$(0,2,0)$};
  \fill (-2,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$(0,-2,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs-multiselect-quadrics">
<title>Which of These Are Hyperboloids?</title>
<statement>
<p>
None of the equations below is in standard position. Complete the square
where necessary, then select <em>every</em> equation whose surface is a
hyperboloid, of one sheet or of two.
</p>
</statement>
<choices randomize="yes">

<choice correct="yes">
<statement><p><m>x^2 + y^2 - z^2 - 2x = 0</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Correct. Completing the square gives
<m>(x-1)^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1</m>: one negative square, equal to a positive
constant, so a hyperboloid of <em>one</em> sheet.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement><p><m>x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 - 4y = 0</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
No. This is <m>x^2 + (y-2)^2 + 4z^2 = 4</m> — every square is positive, so
it is an ellipsoid.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice correct="yes">
<statement><p><m>x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 1</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Correct. <em>Two</em> negative squares against a positive constant gives a
hyperboloid of two sheets.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement><p><m>x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 0</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
No — and this is the trap. It has the same signs as the first option, but
it equals <m>0</m> rather than a positive constant. That degenerates the
hyperboloid into an <em>elliptic cone</em>, the surface the two families of
hyperboloids approach asymptotically.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
<statement><p><m>z + 1 = x^2 - y^2</m></p></statement>
<feedback>
<p>
No. The word <q>hyperbolic</q> is a red herring: this is linear in
<m>z</m>, so it is a <em>hyperbolic paraboloid</em> (a saddle) with vertex
at <m>(0,0,-1)</m>, not a hyperboloid.
</p>
</feedback>
</choice>

</choices>
<solution>
<p>
Completing the square in the first and second,
<md>
<mrow>\amp x^2 + y^2 - z^2 - 2x = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad \Longrightarrow\; (x-1)^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 - 4y = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad \Longrightarrow\; x^2 + (y-2)^2 + 4z^2 = 4 .</mrow>
</md>
Reading off the signs: one negative square against <m>+1</m> is a
hyperboloid of one sheet; two negative squares against <m>+1</m> is a
hyperboloid of two sheets. So the first and third are hyperboloids. The
second is an ellipsoid, the fourth equals <m>0</m> and so is a cone, and
the fifth is linear in <m>z</m> and so is a hyperbolic paraboloid.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-parabolic-cylinder">
<title>Graphing a Parabolic Cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the graph of <m>z = 4 - x^2</m> over the rectangle
<m>-2 \le x \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 4</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
Why is this surface called a <term>parabolic cylinder</term>?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Since the equation <m>z=4-x^2</m> does not involve <m>y</m>, every
cross-section of the surface at a fixed value of <m>y</m> is the
<em>same</em> parabola <m>z=4-x^2</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane, opening
downward from its vertex at <m>(0,4)</m>. Sliding that parabola along
the <m>y</m>-axis from <m>y=0</m> to <m>y=4</m> sweeps out the surface.
</p>
<p>
Over <m>-2\le x\le 2</m>, the parabola satisfies <m>4-x^2 \ge 0</m>,
with equality exactly at <m>x=\pm 2</m>, so the surface touches the
<m>xy</m>-plane along the two edges <m>x=\pm2</m> of the rectangular
region and rises to a maximum height of <m>4</m> along the ridge
<m>x=0</m>. Because it is generated by sliding a fixed curve along a
straight line, rather than curving in the <m>y</m> direction as well,
it is called a <term>parabolic cylinder</term>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-parabolic-cylinder"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-parabolic-cylinder">
<caption>
The parabolic cylinder <m>z=4-x^2</m> over the rectangle
<m>-2\le x\le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>. Every slice at a fixed
<m>y</m> is the same downward-opening parabola.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A curved sheet shaped like a downward opening parabola in the x z
plane, extended without change along the y direction over a rectangular
region in the x y plane, forming an arched, tunnel-like surface.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{110}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.85]
\pgfmathsetmacro{\step}{0.01}
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (2.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-2.5,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-0.5,0);
\draw[thick] (0,0,0.0) -- (0,0,4.0);
% the rectangular region in the xy-plane
\draw[gray,thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.35] (2,0,0) -- (2,4,0) -- (-2,4,0) -- (-2,0,0) -- cycle;
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] (2,0,0) -- (2,4,0) -- (-2,4,0) -- (-2,0,0) -- cycle;
% the curves slicing the surface
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{0},{4.0 - \t*\t});
% the surface
\foreach \y in {0,\step,...,4.0}{
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{\y},{4.0 - \t*\t});
}
% the curves slicing the surface
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},{4.0},{4.0 - \t*\t});
\node[blue,above right] at (0,2.5,4.125) {$z = 4 - x^2$};
% last part of the z axis
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,4.0) -- (0,0,4.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid">
<title>A Solid Bounded by a Cylinder and a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the solid bounded by the three surfaces
<md>
<mrow>
\amp z = 0, \qquad z = x^2+y^2,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp x^2+y^2 = 4.
</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Describe each of the three bounding surfaces.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the solid region they enclose.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe the cross-section of the solid in the plane <m>y=0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> The equation <m>z=0</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane. The
equation <m>z=x^2+y^2</m> is a paraboloid opening upward from its
vertex at the origin. The equation <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> is missing
<m>z</m>, so it is the infinite cylinder of radius <m>2</m> whose axis
is the <m>z</m>-axis. The paraboloid meets that cylinder exactly where
<m>x^2+y^2=4</m>, at height <m>z=4</m>.
</p>
<p>
<term>(b)</term> The solid consists of the points inside the cylinder
lying between the flat base and the paraboloid:
<md>
x^2+y^2 \le 4, \qquad 0 \le z \le x^2+y^2.
</md>
Near the <m>z</m>-axis this range for <m>z</m> is almost nothing, since
<m>x^2+y^2 \approx 0</m> there, while at the rim <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> it is
the full interval <m>0 \le z \le 4</m>. The solid is therefore shaped
like a shallow bowl carved out from underneath the paraboloid: thin at
the center and rising to its full height <m>4</m> at the cylinder wall,
shown in <xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid"/>.
</p>
<p>
<term>(c)</term> Setting <m>y=0</m> restricts the solid's defining
inequalities to <m>-2\le x\le 2</m> and <m>0\le z\le x^2</m>: the
region under the parabola <m>z=x^2</m>, above the <m>x</m>-axis,
between <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=2</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-cross-section"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-cross-section">
<caption>
The cross-section of the solid in the plane <m>y=0</m>: the region
under the parabola <m>z=x^2</m> for <m>-2\le x\le 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="40%">
<description>
<p>
A two dimensional graph in the x z plane. A shaded region lies under
the upward opening parabola z equals x squared, above the x axis,
between x equals negative two and x equals two.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
% the region 0 <= z <= x^2 for -2 <= x <= 2
\fill[cyan!35, opacity=0.85] plot[domain=-2:2, samples=60] (\x,{\x*\x}) -- (2,0) -- (-2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] plot[domain=-2:2, samples=60] (\x,{\x*\x});
\draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-2,0) -- (2,0);
\draw[->, thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$z$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[dashed, gray] (2,0) -- (2,4);
\draw[dashed, gray] (-2,0) -- (-2,4);
\node[below] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[cyan!55!black, above right] at (1.3,2.0) {$z=x^2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-ex3d-cylinder-paraboloid-solid">
<caption>
The solid bounded by <m>z=0</m>, the paraboloid <m>z=x^2+y^2</m>, and
the cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>. The solid fills the region under the
paraboloid, thin near the <m>z</m>-axis and reaching full height where
the paraboloid meets the cylinder wall.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A paraboloid opening upward from the origin, shown as a shaded bowl
sitting inside a cylindrical wall of radius two. The bowl's rim meets
the top of the cylinder, and a shaded disk in the x y plane forms the
flat base of the solid beneath the bowl.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\usetikzlibrary{patterns, math}
\tdplotsetmaincoords{60}{130}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords,scale=0.75]
\tikzmath{function f(\x) {return \x;};}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\zini}{0.5*sqrt(2.0)}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\step}{0.01}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\zsig}{\zini+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\nextz}{\zini+0.5*\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\sig}{2.0*\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tini}{0.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tnext}{\tini+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tfin}{1.85*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tend}{2.5*pi}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\tNext}{\tfin+\step}
\pgfmathsetmacro{\final}{2.0*pi}
% coordinate axes
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (3.5,0,0) node [below left] {\footnotesize$x$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (-2.5,0,0);
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.5,0) node [right] {\footnotesize$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,-2.5,0);
% the base disk
\draw[gray,thick,fill=yellow,opacity=0.35] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{0.0});
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (-2,0,0) -- (-2,0,4);
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (0,-2,0) -- (0,-2,4);
% the cylinder around the paraboloid (back)
\foreach \angulo in {\tini,\tnext,...,\tfin}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[pink,opacity=0.15] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0);
}
% the paraboloid (for z = constant)
\foreach \altura in {\step,\sig,...,4.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=\tini:\tfin,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{\altura});
}
\draw[thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.0);
% the curves bounding the solid
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] ({\t},0,{\t*\t});
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=-2:2,smooth,variable=\t] (0,{\t},{\t*\t});
% the paraboloid (for z = constant)
\foreach \altura in {\step,\sig,...,4.0}{
  \pgfmathparse{sqrt(\altura)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\radio}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[cyan,thick,opacity=0.25] plot[domain=\tfin:\tend,smooth,variable=\t] ({\radio*cos(\t r)},{\radio*sin(\t r)},{\altura});
}
\draw[blue,thick,opacity=0.5] plot[domain=0:6.2832,smooth,variable=\t] ({2.0*cos(\t r)},{2.0*sin(\t r)},{4.0});
% the cylinder out of the paraboloid (front)
\foreach \angulo in {\tfin,\tNext,...,\tend}{
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*cos(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{\pgfmathresult}
  \pgfmathparse{2.0*sin(\angulo r)}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{\pgfmathresult}
  \draw[pink,opacity=0.15] (\px,\py,0) -- (\px,\py,4.0);
}
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (2,0,0) -- (2,0,4);
\draw[gray,dash dot dot] (0,2,0) -- (0,2,4);
\node[blue,left] at (1,-1.25,2.5) {$z = x^2 + y^2$};
\draw[thick,->] (0,0,4.0) -- (0,0,4.5) node [above] {\footnotesize$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== Functions of several variables. ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="q1-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<md>
h(x,y,z) = z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} + 1 .
</md>
Sketch the level surface of <m>h</m> that passes through the point
<m>(3,4,-5)</m>. Clearly label the value of <m>h</m> on that surface,
and label the coordinate axes.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The level surfaces of <m>h</m> are the surfaces <m>h(x,y,z) = c</m>.
To find which one passes through <m>(3,4,-5)</m> we simply evaluate:
<md>
<mrow>h(3,4,-5) \amp = -5 + \sqrt{3^2+4^2} + 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -5 + \sqrt{25} + 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -5 + 5 + 1 = 1 .</mrow>
</md>
So the level surface we want is <m>h(x,y,z) = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now we simplify the equation of that level surface:
<md>
<mrow>z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} + 1 \amp = 1</mrow>
<mrow>z + \sqrt{x^2+y^2} \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>z \amp = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
This is a cone. Indeed, squaring gives <m>z^2 = x^2+y^2</m>, which is
the full double cone; but the equation <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>
forces <m>z \le 0</m>, so we keep only the <em>lower</em> nappe. The
vertex is at the origin, and the horizontal trace at height
<m>z = -k</m> (with <m>k \gt 0</m>) is the circle
<m>x^2+y^2 = k^2</m> of radius <m>k</m>. In other words the cone opens
downwards, and the slope of every straight line drawn on it from the
vertex is <m>-1</m>. The point <m>(3,4,-5)</m> lies on it, since
<m>-\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = -5</m>. See <xref ref="fig-q1-cone"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q1-cone">
<caption>
The level surface <m>h(x,y,z) = 1</m>, that is the downward cone
<m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>. It passes through <m>(3,4,-5)</m>,
which lies on the circle <m>x^2+y^2=25</m> in the plane <m>z=-5</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A cone with its vertex at the origin opening downwards around the
negative z axis. The horizontal circle of radius five is drawn on it in
the plane z equals negative five, and the point three, four, negative
five is marked on that circle.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=11cm, height=9.5cm,
  view={-35}{18},
  xmin=-6.5, xmax=6.5, ymin=-6.5, ymax=6.5, zmin=-6.5, zmax=2.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-5,0,5}, ytick={-5,0,5}, ztick={-5,0},
  clip=false,
]
% the cone z = -sqrt(x^2+y^2), drawn in polar form
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=mblue, draw=mblue,
          fill opacity=0.30, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:5.6, y domain=0:360, samples=12, samples y=41,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{-x});
% the trace circle at z = -5
\addplot3[very thick, mverm, domain=0:360, samples=60]
  ({5*cos(x)},{5*sin(x)},{-5});
% the marked point (3,4,-5)
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2pt, mred] coordinates {(3,4,-5)};
\node[mred, anchor=west, fill=white, fill opacity=0.75, text opacity=1,
      inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:3,4,-5) {$(3,4,-5)$};
\node[mverm, anchor=north] at (axis cs:0,-5.6,-5.6)
  {$x^2+y^2=25$};
\node[mblue, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:0.72,0.86)
  {$h=1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q2-1">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch three level curves of
<md>
h(x,y) = \frac{y^2}{x} ,
</md>
including the one that passes through the point <m>(-1,2)</m>. Clearly
label the value of <m>h</m> on each curve.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First we find the level through <m>(-1,2)</m>:
<md>
h(-1,2) = \frac{2^2}{-1} = -4 .
</md>
So one of the three curves must be <m>h(x,y) = -4</m>.
</p>

<p>
In general the level curve <m>h(x,y) = c</m> is
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \frac{y^2}{x} = c \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad y^2 = c\,x
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x = \frac{y^2}{c} \quad (c \ne 0) .
</mrow>
</md>
Each of these is a <em>parabola opening along the</em> <m>x</m><em>-axis</em>,
with vertex at the origin. If <m>c \gt 0</m> it opens to the right, and
if <m>c \lt 0</m> it opens to the left. Note that the domain of
<m>h</m> excludes <m>x = 0</m>, so the vertex itself, the origin, must
be removed from every level curve.
</p>

<p>
Let us take the three levels <m>c = -4</m>, <m>c = 1</m> and
<m>c = 4</m>:
<md>
<mrow>h = -4: \amp \quad x = -\frac{y^2}{4} \quad \text{(opens left)},</mrow>
<mrow>h = 1: \amp \quad x = y^2 \quad \text{(opens right, narrow)},</mrow>
<mrow>h = 4: \amp \quad x = \frac{y^2}{4} \quad \text{(opens right, wide)}.</mrow>
</md>
The larger <m>|c|</m> is, the wider the parabola. These are drawn in
<xref ref="fig-q2-levels"/>.
</p>

<p>
One more remark: the level <m>c = 0</m> is special. There
<m>y^2 = 0</m>, so the level curve <m>h = 0</m> is not a parabola at
all but the <m>x</m>-axis with the origin removed.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-q2-levels">
<caption>
Three level curves of <m>h(x,y) = y^2/x</m>. The curve through
<m>(-1,2)</m> is the level <m>h = -4</m>, which opens to the left. The
origin is excluded from every level curve, since <m>h</m> is undefined
when <m>x = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
Three parabolas with vertices at the origin and axes along the x axis.
One opens to the left and is labelled h equals negative four; two open
to the right and are labelled h equals one and h equals four. The point
negative one comma two is marked on the leftward parabola.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-q2-levels">
<diagram dimensions="(430,380)" margins="30">
<definition>cm4(t) = (-t^2/4, t)</definition>
<definition>c1(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t^2/4, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-3.4,4.6,3.9)">
<grid-axes at="q2l-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-m4" function="cm4" domain="(-3.2,3.2)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-1" function="c1" domain="(-2.0,2.0)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="q2l-4" function="c4" domain="(-3.2,3.2)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="q2l-P" p="(-1,2)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="q2l-Plab" anchor="(-1.20,2.18)" alignment="nw" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>(-1,2)</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-m4lab" anchor="(-2.9,3.45)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>h=-4</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-1lab" anchor="(4.45,1.60)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>h=1</m>
</label>
<label at="q2l-4lab" anchor="(3.35,3.45)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>h=4</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three parabolas sharing the origin as vertex and the x axis as axis of symmetry. An orange one opens to the left, labelled h equals negative four. A blue narrow one and a green wide one open to the right, labelled h equals one and h equals four. A dark red dot marks the point negative one comma two on the orange curve.">
  <annotation ref="q2l-m4" text="The level curve h equals negative four, the parabola x equals minus y squared over four, opening to the left."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-1" text="The level curve h equals one, the parabola x equals y squared, opening to the right."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-4" text="The level curve h equals four, the parabola x equals y squared over four, opening to the right and wider than the previous one."/>
  <annotation ref="q2l-P" text="The point negative one comma two, which lies on the level curve h equals negative four."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-lim-1-b">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
To show that the limit at a point <m>(a,b)</m> exists, it suffices to
find two paths to the point <m>(a,b)</m> where the limits of
<m>f(x,y)</m> agree.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
We would have to show the limit agrees on
<em>every</em> path, but this is not feasible.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-lim-2">
<statement>
<p>
Show that the limit does not exist:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,0)} \frac{x + y^2}{(x-1)^3 + y^4}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Approaching the limit along the <m>x</m>-axis, i.e. <m>y = 0</m>, we
need to consider
<md>
\lim_{x\to 1} \frac{x}{(x-1)^3},
</md>
which does not exist since the numerator has limit <m>1</m> but the
denominator has limit <m>0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-3">
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the following limit or show that the limit does not exist:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{x^2 + y^4}{x^2 + y^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Along the line <m>y = x</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{x^2 + y^4}{x^2 + y^2} = \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^2 + x^4}{2x^2}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \lim_{x\to 0} \frac12\left(1 + x^2\right) = \frac12.
</mrow>
</md>
Along the line <m>y = 0</m>:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{x^2 + y^4}{x^2 + y^2}
= \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{x^2}{x^2} = 1.
</md>
Since <m>1 \ne \frac12</m>, the limit does not exist.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-6">
<statement>
<p>
Show that the following limit does not exist:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{2x^2 y}{x^4 + y^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Along <m>x = 0</m>, we have
<md>
f(0,y) = \frac{0}{0 + y^2} = 0,
</md>
hence <m>f(x,y)</m> approaches zero as <m>(x,y)\to(0,0)</m> along the
line <m>x = 0</m>. Along <m>y = x^2</m>, we have
<md>
f\left(x, x^2\right) = \frac{2x^4}{x^4 + x^4} = \frac{x^4}{x^4} = 1,
</md>
and hence <m>f(x,y) \to 1</m> as <m>(x,y)\to(0,0)</m> along
<m>y = x^2</m>. Since the limits computed along two different paths
are not equal, we can conclude that
<m>\lim\limits_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-8-c">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If two functions <m>f(x,y)</m> and <m>g(x,y)</m> are both continuous
at the point <m>P_0</m>, then the quotient <m>\dfrac{f}{g}</m> is also
continuous at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Counterexample:
<md>
f(x,y) = x + y
\quad\text{and}\quad
g(x,y) = x^2 - y^2
</md>
are both continuous at <m>(0,0)</m>. However,
<md>
\frac{f}{g} = \frac{x+y}{x^2 - y^2} = \frac{1}{x-y}
</md>
is <em>not</em> continuous at <m>(0,0)</m>, since for example along
<m>y = 0</m>,
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} \frac{1}{x-y}
= \lim_{x\to 0^{\pm}} \frac{1}{x} = \pm\infty.
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Partial Derivatives" ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="q2-3">
<statement>
<p>
A metal plate occupies the square <m>0 \le x \le 5</m>,
<m>0 \le y \le 5</m>, and is heated so that the temperature at the
point <m>(x,y)</m> is <m>T(x,y)</m>, measured in degrees Fahrenheit.
Distances are measured in feet. The table below records some measured
values of <m>T</m>.
</p>

<table xml:id="tab-q2-temps">
<title>Measured temperatures <m>T(x,y)</m> in <m>{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="medium" left="medium">
<row bottom="medium">
<cell></cell><cell right="medium"><m>y=1</m></cell><cell><m>y=1.5</m></cell><cell><m>y=2</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>70</m></cell><cell><m>73</m></cell><cell><m>79</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=1.5</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>65</m></cell><cell><m>69</m></cell><cell><m>75</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>x=2</m></cell><cell right="medium"><m>58</m></cell><cell><m>60</m></cell><cell><m>65</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_x(1,1)</m> and interpret your answer, with units, in
the context of the problem.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Approximate <m>T_{xy}(1,1)</m>, and give its units.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
An ant walks across the plate along the path
<md>
<mrow>
\amp x = 1 - \frac{\ln(2t-3)}{4}, \qquad y = \frac{2^t}{4},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp 1.5 \lt t \lt 4 .
</mrow>
</md>
Approximate the rate at which the temperature the ant feels is
changing at the moment it is at the point <m>(x,y) = (1,1)</m>, and
give the units.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> Holding <m>y=1</m> and stepping forward in <m>x</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\amp T_x(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1) - T(1,1)}{0.5}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac{65-70}{0.5} = -10 \quad {}^{\circ}\text{F}/\text{ft} .
</mrow>
</md>
So at <m>(1,1)</m>, moving in the positive <m>x</m>-direction with
<m>y</m> held fixed, the temperature <em>drops</em> at a rate of about
<m>10\,{}^{\circ}\text{F}</m> per foot.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> We need <m>T_x</m> at a second value of <m>y</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\amp T_x(1,1.5) \approx \frac{T(1.5,1.5) - T(1,1.5)}{0.5}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac{69-73}{0.5} = -8 .
</mrow>
</md>
Then
<md>
<mrow>
\amp T_{xy}(1,1) \approx \frac{T_x(1,1.5) - T_x(1,1)}{0.5}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac{-8-(-10)}{0.5} = 4 \quad {}^{\circ}\text{F}/\text{ft}^2 .
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> As in <xref ref="q1-3"/>, the ant is at
<m>(1,1)</m> when <m>\ln(2t-3) = 0</m>, that is at <m>t = 2</m>, and
then <m>y = 2^2/4 = 1</m> as required. Differentiating the path,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt} \amp = -\frac{1}{4}\cdot\frac{2}{2t-3} = -\frac{1}{2(2t-3)},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dx}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = -\frac{1}{2} ,</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = \frac{2^t\ln 2}{4},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dy}{dt}\bigg|_{t=2} \amp = \ln 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
From the table,
<md>
<mrow>
\amp T_y(1,1) \approx \frac{T(1,1.5)-T(1,1)}{0.5}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac{73-70}{0.5} = 6 .
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
By the chain rule,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dT}{dt} \amp = T_x\,\frac{dx}{dt} + T_y\,\frac{dy}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \approx (-10)\left(-\frac12\right) + (6)(\ln 2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 5 + 6\ln 2 \approx 9.16 ,</mrow>
</md>
in degrees Fahrenheit per unit of <m>t</m>. Here the ant is
<em>warming up</em>: it moves in the <m>-x</m> direction, where the
plate is hotter, and in the <m>+y</m> direction, where the plate is
also hotter, so both contributions are positive. Compare this with
<xref ref="q1-3"/>, where the same computation gave a negative rate.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for The Chain Rule" ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="pp-29">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,z) = x^2z + e^{xz + z^2}</m> and
<m>x = r\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>z = r\sin(\theta)</m>. Use the chain rule
to compute <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial \theta}</m> at
<m>(r,\theta) = \left(3, \frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The chain rule gives
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial \theta}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\frac{\partial z}{\partial \theta}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left[2xz + z\,e^{xz+z^2}\right](-r\sin\theta)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \left[x^2 + (x + 2z)e^{xz+z^2}\right](r\cos\theta).</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
At <m>(r,\theta) = \left(3, \frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> we have
<m>x = 3\cos\frac{\pi}{2} = 0</m> and
<m>z = 3\sin\frac{\pi}{2} = 3</m>, so <m>(x,z) = (0,3)</m> and
<m>xz + z^2 = 0 + 9 = 9</m>. Also
<m>-r\sin\theta = -3</m> and <m>r\cos\theta = 0</m>. Hence
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta} = \left[0 + 3e^{9}\right](-3) + \left[0 + 6e^{9}\right](0)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = -9e^{9}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Directional Derivatives
     and Gradient Vectors": its even-numbered problems ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find all points at which the direction of fastest change of the
function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 4y</m> is
<m>\langle 1, 1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The direction of fastest change is
<m>\nabla f(x,y) = \langle 2x-2,\, 2y-4\rangle</m>, so we need to
find all points <m>(x,y)</m> such that
<m>\nabla f(x,y) = k\langle 1,1\rangle</m>. Then
<md>
2x - 2 = k \quad\text{and}\quad 2y - 4 = k,
</md>
so
<md>
2x - 2 = 2y - 4 \;\Rightarrow\; y = x + 1.
</md>
Hence, the direction of fastest change is <m>\langle 1,1\rangle</m>
at all points on the line <m>y = x + 1</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-gr-line"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-gr-line">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f(x,y) = x^2+y^2-2x-4y</m>, which are circles
centered at <m>(1,2)</m>. Along the line <m>y = x+1</m> the gradient
<m>\nabla f</m> points in the direction <m>\langle 1,1\rangle</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rev-gr-line">
<diagram dimensions="(340,320)" margins="12">
<definition>c1(t) = (1 + 0.8*cos(t), 2 + 0.8*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (1 + 1.6*cos(t), 2 + 1.6*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>c3(t) = (1 + 2.4*cos(t), 2 + 2.4*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.1,-1.1,4.3,5.1)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="gr-c1" function="c1" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="gr-c2" function="c2" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="gr-c3" function="c3" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="gr-line" endpoints="((-1.9,-0.9),(3.9,4.9))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<point at="gr-center" p="(1,2)" fill="#7F7F7F"/>
<vector at="gr-v1" v="(0.75,0.75)" tail="(1.566,2.566)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gr-v2" v="(0.75,0.75)" tail="(2.131,3.131)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gr-v3" v="(0.75,0.75)" tail="(2.697,3.697)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="gr-line-lab" anchor="(-1.05,-0.95)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>y=x+1</m>
</label>
<label at="gr-grad-lab" anchor="(2.30,-0.78)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f \parallel \langle 1,1\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="gr-center-lab" anchor="(1.12,1.82)" alignment="se" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>(1,2)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three concentric blue circles centered at the point one comma two are level curves of f. A dark red line with slope one, y equals x plus one, passes through the center. At three points on this line outside the smallest circle, orange arrows of equal length point up and to the right in the direction one comma one, perpendicular to the circles.">
  <annotation ref="gr-c1" text="A level curve of f: a circle centered at one comma two."/>
  <annotation ref="gr-line" text="The line y equals x plus one, through the center of the circles, where the gradient points in the direction one comma one."/>
  <annotation ref="gr-v1" text="The gradient direction one comma one at a point on the line, perpendicular to the level curves."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
<m>D_{\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}}\,f(x,y) = f_x + f_y</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Since
<m>\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath} = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\amp D_{\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}}\,f(x,y) = \nabla f(x,y) \cdot \langle 1, 1\rangle
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = f_x + f_y.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-6">
<statement>
<p>
A metal plate is placed on the <m>xy</m>-plane in such a way that the
temperature <m>T</m> at any point <m>(x,y)</m> is given by
<m>T = e^x(\sin x + \sin y)</m> <degree/>C.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
What is the rate of change in temperature at <m>(0,0)</m> in the
direction of <m>3\,\hat{\imath} - 4\,\hat{\jmath}</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(0,0)</m>, in what direction is the temperature increasing most
rapidly?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(0,0)</m>, in what direction is the temperature decreasing most
rapidly?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We first compute the gradient:
<md>
<mrow>T_x \amp = e^x(\sin x + \sin y) + e^x\cos x</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = e^x(\sin x + \cos x + \sin y),</mrow>
<mrow>T_x(0,0) \amp = 1,</mrow>
<mrow>T_y \amp = e^x \cos y,</mrow>
<mrow>T_y(0,0) \amp = 1,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)} = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>. The direction
vector is <m>\overrightarrow{u} = \langle 3, -4\rangle</m> with
<m>\|\overrightarrow{u}\| = \sqrt{9 + 16} = 5</m>, so the unit vector is
<m>\hat u = \dfrac{\overrightarrow{u}}{\|\overrightarrow{u}\|}
= \left\langle \dfrac35, -\dfrac45\right\rangle</m>. Hence
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \left(D_{\hat u} T\right)_{(0,0)} = \nabla T\big|_{(0,0)} \cdot \hat u
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \langle 1, 1\rangle \cdot \left\langle \frac35, -\frac45\right\rangle = -\frac15.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature increases most rapidly in the direction of the
gradient:
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}}{\left\|\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}\right\|}
= \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to
the gradient:
<md>
\hat u = -\frac{\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}}{\left\|\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}\right\|}
= \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-7">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the maximum rate of change of <m>f(x,y) = \ln(x^2+y^2)</m>
at the point <m>(-4, 3)</m> and determine the direction in which it
occurs.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
\nabla f = \left(\frac{2x}{x^2+y^2}\right)\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{2y}{x^2+y^2}\right)\hat{\jmath},
</md>
so at <m>(-4,3)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f(-4,3)
\amp = \left(-\frac{8}{25}\right)\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{6}{25}\right)\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\|\nabla f(-4,3)\|
\amp = \sqrt{\frac{100}{(25)^2}} = \frac{10}{25}
= \frac25.</mrow>
</md>
The direction of maximum increase is
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla f(-4,3)}{\|\nabla f(-4,3)\|}
= \left(-\frac45\right)\hat{\imath} + \left(\frac35\right)\hat{\jmath},
</md>
and the maximum rate of increase is
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} = \|\nabla f(-4,3)\| = \frac25.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-7">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the directions in which <m>f(x,y)</m> increases and decreases
most rapidly at <m>P_0(-1,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the derivatives of <m>f(x,y)</m> in these directions at
<m>P_0(-1,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = (2x + y)\,\hat{\imath} + (x + 2y)\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)} \amp = \langle -1, 1\rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\| \amp = \sqrt2.</mrow>
</md>
The function increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient,
<md>
\hat u_1 = \frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}}
{\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|}
= \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle,
</md>
and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction,
<md>
\hat u_2 = -\frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}}
{\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|}
= \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The directional derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>\amp \left(D_{\hat u_1} f\right)_{P_0} = \left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)} \cdot \hat u_1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \frac{1}{\sqrt2} + \frac{1}{\sqrt2} = \sqrt2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \left(D_{\hat u_2} f\right)_{P_0} = \left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)} \cdot \hat u_2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = -\frac{1}{\sqrt2} - \frac{1}{\sqrt2} = -\sqrt2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = -\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|.</mrow>
</md>
As expected, the largest possible rate of change is
<m>\|\nabla f\|</m> and the smallest is <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-30">
<statement>
<p>
In the <m>xy</m>-plane, the temperature at the point <m>(x,y)</m> is
given by <m>T(x,y) = 3x^2 + 8xy + y^2</m>. You're at <m>(1,0)</m>
where the temperature is <m>3^{\circ}</m>. Brrrr!
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
In what direction should you go, from <m>(1,0)</m>, in order to warm
up the fastest?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What is the directional derivative in that direction at the point
<m>(1,0)</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If you walk <m>0.2</m> units in that direction, by about how many
degrees will you warm up?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
A function increases fastest in the direction of its gradient:
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \nabla T = \langle 6x + 8y,\; 8x + 2y\rangle,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \nabla T\big|_{(1,0)} = \langle 6, 8\rangle = 6\,\hat{\imath} + 8\,\hat{\jmath}.
</mrow>
</md>
So head in the direction <m>6\,\hat{\imath} + 8\,\hat{\jmath}</m>
(equivalently, the unit vector
<m>\left\langle \frac35, \frac45\right\rangle</m>).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
In the direction of the gradient, the directional derivative equals
the length of the gradient:
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \left(D_{\hat u}T\right)_{(1,0)} = \left\|\nabla T\big|_{(1,0)}\right\| = \sqrt{36 + 64}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = 10.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The directional derivative is the rate of change of temperature per
unit of distance travelled, so by the linear approximation
<md>
\Delta T \approx \left(D_{\hat u}T\right) \cdot s
= 10 \times 0.2 = 2^{\circ}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-32">
<statement>
<p>
A sheet of metal of varying density occupies the <m>xy</m>-plane. At
the point <m>(x,y)</m>, the density is
<m>100 + 0.1xy + 0.1y^2</m>. We move away from the point
<m>(10,10)</m> in the direction
<m>\frac35\hat{\imath} + \frac45\hat{\jmath}</m>. Use the directional
derivative to estimate about how far we'll have to go for the density
to increase by <m>6</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>\rho(x,y) = 100 + 0.1xy + 0.1y^2</m>. Then
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \nabla \rho = \langle 0.1y,\; 0.1x + 0.2y\rangle,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \nabla \rho\big|_{(10,10)} = \langle 1,\; 1 + 2\rangle = \langle 1, 3\rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
The given direction is already a unit vector, since
<m>\left(\frac35\right)^2 + \left(\frac45\right)^2 = 1</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>
\amp D_{\hat u}\rho(10,10) = \langle 1, 3\rangle \cdot \left\langle \frac35, \frac45\right\rangle
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac35 + \frac{12}{5} = 3.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
So the density increases at about <m>3</m> units of density per unit
of distance. To gain <m>6</m> units of density we therefore need to
travel approximately
<md>
s \approx \frac{6}{3} = 2 \text{ units}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<p>
The problem that closes this set repeats the three questions of
<xref ref="ex-skel-two-runners"/> in a different physical setting: find the
direction of most rapid decrease, the rate along a prescribed direction,
and the directions of no change.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-fall-line">
  <title>The Fall Line on a Ski Slope</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A mountainside has elevation, in feet,
      <md>h(x,y) = 3000 - 0.0004x^2 - 0.001y^2,</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in feet. A skier stands at <m>P_0(200,100)</m>. Her
      gravitational potential energy is <m>U = mgh</m>, so the downhill force she feels is
      a positive multiple of <m>-\nabla h</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the <term>fall line</term>, the unit vector of steepest descent, and the
            grade of the slope there, expressed as a percent (feet dropped per hundred feet
            travelled).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A traverse is a route on which she neither climbs nor descends. Find the two
            unit vectors that accomplish this.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            She wants a gentler line of exactly <m>12\%</m> grade. Using
            <m>D_{\hat{u}}h = \|\nabla h\|\cos\theta</m>, find the angle <m>\theta</m>
            between her heading and the fall line.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nabla h = -0.0008x\,\hat{\imath} - 0.002y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
            <m>(\nabla h)_{(200,100)} = -0.16\,\hat{\imath} - 0.20\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. Steepest
            descent is along
            <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{41}}(4\,\hat{\imath} + 5\,\hat{\jmath})</m>, and
            <m>\|\nabla h\| = \sqrt{0.0656} \approx 0.256</m>, a grade of about
            <m>25.6\%</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\hat{u} = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{41}}(5\,\hat{\imath} - 4\,\hat{\jmath})</m>, tangent
            to the contour line of constant elevation through <m>P_0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\cos\theta = 0.12/0.256 \approx 0.469</m>, so
            <m>\theta \approx 62^\circ</m> off the fall line, on either side.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
    <p component="print">
      Two interactive figures accompany the online version of these notes:
      one shows the grade as the angle <m>\alpha</m> of the tangent line cut
      by a swinging vertical plane through <m>P_0</m>, and the other compares
      the fall line, the level traverse, and the <m>12\%</m> line on the
      mountainside itself.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-alpha-tangent">
      <caption>
        Part (a) says the grade is <m>\|\nabla h\| = 0.2561</m>. Here is what that
        number is, on the hill itself. The vertical plane through <m>P_0</m> in the
        direction <m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the surface in the gold curve; the red line
        is that curve's tangent at <m>P_0</m>; and <m>\alpha</m> is the angle it
        makes with the horizontal. Since <m>\tan\alpha = D_{\hat{u}}h</m>, the
        grade <em>is</em> the tangent of that angle. Swing <m>\hat{u}</m> with the
        slider: <m>\alpha</m> is largest along the fall line, where
        <m>\tan\alpha = 0.2561</m> and <m>\alpha = 14.4^\circ</m>, and it closes to
        nothing along the contour, where the tangent line lies flat.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-alpha-tangent"
                   iframe="alpha-tangent.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="1:1"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-fall-line">
      <caption>The mountainside <m>h(x,y) = 3000 - 0.0004x^2 - 0.001y^2</m>. Compare the fall line, the level traverse, and the <m>12\%</m> line. Watch the grade readout drift away from <m>12\%</m> as the skier leaves <m>P_0</m>, since the angle in part (c) is computed at <m>P_0</m> only.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-fall-line"
                   iframe="fall-line-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== Moved from Assignment 9 (Nov 2026): a contour map and two physical
     settings for the gradient, then three tangent-plane problems ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="pp-31">
<statement>
<p>
<m>f</m> is a <q>nice</q> function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> (i.e.
<m>f</m> is continuous and its partials exist). In
<xref ref="fig-pp-contours"/> are shown the level curves
<m>f(x,y) = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Let <m>u_1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Estimate <m>D_{u_1}f(4,3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate <m>D_{u_1}f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Draw on the diagram a unit vector pointing in the same direction as
<m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>P</m>, draw a unit vector and label it <m>u_2</m>, such that
<m>D_{u_2}f(P) \lt 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(P)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Which appears bigger, <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q)</m> or
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(R)</m>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-contours">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f</m> at the values
<m>0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</m>. The marked point <m>P</m> is the highest
point of the <m>f = 12</m> curve; <m>Q</m> lies midway between the
<m>f = 2</m> and <m>f = 4</m> curves; <m>R</m> lies midway between the
<m>f = 10</m> and <m>f = 12</m> curves.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-contours">
<diagram dimensions="(470,420)" margins="12">
<definition>c0(t) = (t, -0.30 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (t, -0.08 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t, 0.17 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c6(t) = (t, 0.75 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c8(t) = (t, 1.88 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c10(t) = (t, 2.98 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c12(t) = (t, 3.90 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.35,8.3,6.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-0" function="c0" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-2" function="c2" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-4" function="c4" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-6" function="c6" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-8" function="c8" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-10" function="c10" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-12" function="c12" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<!-- P is the peak of the f=12 curve; Q is midway between f=2 and f=4,
     R midway between f=10 and f=12, so each point's position is exact. -->
<point at="ct-P" p="(2.4,5.697)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ct-Q" p="(5.2,0.59)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ct-R" p="(4.6,4.298)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<line at="ct-Q-lead" endpoints="((5.24,0.64),(5.42,0.86))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="1.5"/>
<label at="ct-P-lab" anchor="(2.4,5.82)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-Q-lab" anchor="(5.45,0.90)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>Q</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-R-lab" anchor="(4.72,4.30)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>R</m>
</label>
<!-- The f=2 and f=0 labels do not fit beside their curves, so each is
     set below and joined to its curve by a leader. -->
<line at="ct-lead2" endpoints="((6.35,0.114),(6.70,-0.41))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1"/>
<line at="ct-lead0" endpoints="((6.35,-0.106),(6.70,-1.01))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1"/>
<label at="ct-l12" anchor="(6.45,4.094)" alignment="e">
<m>f=12</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l10" anchor="(6.45,3.174)" alignment="e">
<m>f=10</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l8" anchor="(6.45,2.074)" alignment="e">
<m>f=8</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l6" anchor="(6.45,0.944)" alignment="e">
<m>f=6</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l4" anchor="(6.45,0.364)" alignment="e">
<m>f=4</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l2" anchor="(6.75,-0.45)" alignment="e">
<m>f=2</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l0" anchor="(6.75,-1.05)" alignment="e">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Seven level curves of f, labelled zero through twelve from the bottom up. Each curve rises steeply from the left, reaches a hump near x equals two point four, and then descends gently to the right. The low valued curves are bunched tightly together near the x axis on the right, where the point Q sits, while the high valued curves are widely spaced near the top right, where the point R sits. The point P sits at the very top of the f equals twelve curve, where that curve is horizontal.">
  <annotation ref="ct-P" text="The point P, on the f equals twelve level curve at its highest point, where that curve is horizontal."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-Q" text="The point Q, at the lower right, exactly midway between the f equals two and f equals four level curves, which are only about one quarter of a unit apart."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-R" text="The point R, at the upper right, exactly midway between the f equals ten and f equals twelve level curves, which are about one unit apart."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The directional derivative <m>D_{u_1}f(4,3)</m> tells us roughly how
much <m>f</m> increases if we move <em>one unit</em> from
<m>(4,3)</m> in the direction <m>u_1</m>, i.e. diagonally up and to
the right at <m>45^{\circ}</m>. Starting at <m>(4,3)</m> and moving
one unit that way lands us near <m>(4.7, 3.7)</m>, which is about two
contours further along, and each contour step is <m>\Delta f = 2</m>
spread over roughly one unit of travel. Reading the map,
<md>
D_{u_1}f(4,3) \approx 2.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(1,2)</m> the level curve through that point is climbing at
roughly <m>45^{\circ}</m>, i.e. the level curve is
<em>parallel</em> to <m>u_1</m>. Moving along a level curve does not
change the value of <m>f</m>, so
<md>
D_{u_1}f(1,2) \approx 0.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The gradient is perpendicular to the level curve and points in the
direction of <em>increase</em>. At <m>(1,2)</m> the level curve runs
up and to the right at about <m>45^{\circ}</m>, and <m>f</m>
increases as we move upward, so <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m> points up and to
the <em>left</em>, i.e. in the direction
<m>\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle -1, 1\rangle</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-contours-answer"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We need any direction of <em>decrease</em> at <m>P</m>, i.e. any
direction pointing toward the lower-valued level curves (toward
<m>f = 10</m>, not away from it). Since the level curves below
<m>P</m> have smaller values, <m>u_2 = \langle 0, -1\rangle</m>
(straight down) works. See <xref ref="fig-pp-contours-answer"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>P</m> sits at the very top of its level curve, where the curve is
<em>horizontal</em>. Walking in the <m>+x</m> direction from
<m>P</m> therefore keeps us on the same level curve for a while, so
<m>f</m> does not change to first order:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(P) \approx 0.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> measures how fast <m>f</m>
changes as we move straight up, which is large where the contours are
close together and small where they are far apart. At <m>Q</m> we only
have to go up about <m>0.25</m> units to move from one contour to the
next, so
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q) \approx \dfrac{2}{0.25} = 8</m>.
At <m>R</m> we must go up about <m>1</m> unit for the same change, so
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(R) \approx \dfrac{2}{1} = 2</m>.
Hence <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q)</m> is bigger.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-contours-answer">
<caption>
The vectors asked for in parts C and D: <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m> points
perpendicular to the level curve toward increasing <m>f</m>, while
<m>u_2</m> at <m>P</m> points toward decreasing <m>f</m>.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-contours-answer">
<diagram dimensions="(470,420)" margins="12">
<definition>c0(t) = (t, -0.30 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (t, -0.08 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t, 0.17 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c6(t) = (t, 0.75 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c8(t) = (t, 1.88 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c10(t) = (t, 2.98 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c12(t) = (t, 3.90 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.35,8.3,6.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-0" function="c0" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
