</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<p>
The next two problems repeat the three questions of
<xref ref="ex-skel-two-runners"/> in two more physical settings: find the
direction of most rapid decrease, the rate along a prescribed direction,
and the directions of no change.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-electrostatic-plate">
  <title>Electric Potential on a Coated Plate</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The electric potential on a thin coated plate, in volts, is
      <md>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2,</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in centimeters. Consider the point <m>P_0(2,1)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find <m>\nabla V</m> at <m>P_0</m> and the electric field
            <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m> there. Along which unit vector does the
            potential drop most rapidly, and at what rate?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A test charge is nudged along
            <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{4}{5}\hat{\imath} - \tfrac{3}{5}\hat{\jmath}</m>. Compute
            <m>D_{\hat{u}}V</m> at <m>P_0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the two unit vectors along which the potential does not change, and name
            the curve they are tangent to.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nabla V = -4x\,\hat{\imath} - 12y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
            <m>(\nabla V)_{(2,1)} = -8\,\hat{\imath} - 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> and
            <m>\mathbf E = 8\,\hat{\imath} + 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> V/cm. The potential drops
            fastest along <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath})</m>,
            at the rate <m>-\|\nabla V\| = -4\sqrt{13} \approx -14.4</m> V/cm.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>
<mrow>
\amp D_{\hat{u}}V = (-8)\left(\tfrac{4}{5}\right) + (-12)\left(-\tfrac{3}{5}\right) = \tfrac{4}{5}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = 0.8
</mrow>
</md>
            in V/cm. The potential rises slightly along this heading.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\hat{u} = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(3\,\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. These are
            tangent to the equipotential ellipse <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m> through <m>P_0</m>,
            and they are orthogonal to <m>\mathbf E</m>, as they must be.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An interactive figure of the plate <mdash/> the equipotential route at
      <m>V = 86</m> V and the field-line route side by side, with the
      potential read out as the point moves <mdash/> accompanies the online
      version of these notes.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-potential-plate">
      <caption>The potential <m>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2</m> on the plate. One route holds <m>V = 86</m> V along the equipotential <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m>; the other follows a field line, on which <m>D_{\hat{u}}V = -\|\nabla V\|</m> at every point.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-potential-plate"
                   iframe="potential-plate-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-isobars-low">
  <title>Sailing the Isobars of a Low</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Near the center of a low-pressure system the sea-level pressure, in millibars, is
      modeled by
      <md>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004\left(x^2 + y^2\right),</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in miles from the center of the low. A ship sits at
      <m>P_0(20,10)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the pressure at the ship and <m>\nabla P</m> there. The
            pressure-gradient force per unit volume of air is a positive multiple of
            <m>-\nabla P</m>; in which unit direction does it push, and what is
            <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> in millibars per mile?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A second ship 40 miles from the center reports a stronger wind. Using this
            model, explain why <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> supports that report.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The captain decides to hold a constant barometer reading. Give the two
            possible unit headings at <m>P_0</m>, and describe the shape of the full route.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>P(20,10) = 988 + 0.004(500) = 990</m> mb, and
            <m>\nabla P = 0.008x\,\hat{\imath} + 0.008y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> gives
            <m>(\nabla P)_{(20,10)} = 0.16\,\hat{\imath} + 0.08\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. The force
            points along
            <m>-\mathbf u = -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath})</m>, that is,
            inward toward the low, and
            <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.08\sqrt{5} \approx 0.18</m> mb/mi.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Here <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> grows linearly with distance from
            the center, so at 40 miles it is <m>0.32</m> mb/mi, nearly double the value at
            the first ship. (Real storms reverse this trend close to the eye; the model is
            only reasonable over a limited annulus.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf u = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. Since the
            level curves of <m>P</m> are the circles <m>x^2+y^2 = \text{constant}</m>,
            holding the barometer steady means circling the storm at a fixed radius of
            <m>\sqrt{500} \approx 22.4</m> miles.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="web">
      Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
      switching between the two headings and watching how the barometer responds as
      the ship moves.
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An interactive figure of the pressure field <mdash/> the two headings
      switchable, with the barometer responding as the ship moves <mdash/>
      accompanies the online version of these notes.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-isobars-low">
      <caption>The pressure field <m>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004(x^2+y^2)</m> around a low. One heading circles the eye at constant <m>990</m> mb; the other runs outward along <m>\nabla P</m>. The distance readout shows why <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008r</m> grows with radius.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isobars-low"
                   iframe="isobars-low-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find a parametric equation for a line contained in the tangent plane
to the graph of <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy)</m> at the point
<m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m>. (Any line will suffice.)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The tangent plane to the graph of <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy)</m> at this point
is <m>x + \frac{\pi}{2}\,y + z = \pi</m>. Note that the point
<m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m> lies on this plane, and the plane has normal vector
<m>\vec n = \left\langle 1, \dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1\right\rangle</m>. Any
vector orthogonal to the normal vector will point along this plane. By
inspection, we see that
<md>
\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle \cdot
\left\langle 1, \frac{\pi}{2}, 1\right\rangle = 0,
</md>
so the vector <m>\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle</m> points along the plane.
Thus <m>\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle</m> is the direction vector of a line
pointing along this plane. Combining this with the point on the plane
that we have already found, we see that the line
<md>
\mathbf r(t) = \left\langle t + \frac{\pi}{2},\; 1,\; -t\right\rangle
</md>
is contained in the tangent plane. We could find other lines contained
in the plane by making different choices of direction vector. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-tp-line"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-tp-line">
<caption>
The tangent plane <m>x + \frac{\pi}{2}y + z = \pi</m> to the graph of
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy)</m> at <m>P_0\left(\frac{\pi}{2},1,0\right)</m>,
its normal vector <m>\vec n = \langle 1, \frac{\pi}{2}, 1\rangle</m>,
and the line <m>\mathbf r(t) = \langle t + \frac{\pi}{2}, 1, -t\rangle</m>
contained in the plane.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system showing a tilted shaded
parallelogram that represents the tangent plane. A red point P zero at
pi over two comma one comma zero lies in the middle of the plane. A
blue arrow perpendicular to the plane at P zero is the normal vector
n, and a dark red line through P zero lying inside the plane is the
line r of t.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,2.9,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,0,3.2) node[above] {$z$};
  % tangent plane patch: z = pi - x - (pi/2) y over x in [0.6,2.4], y in [0.3,1.7]
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.30]
    (0.6,0.3,2.0705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,0.3,0.2705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,1.7,-1.9285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0.6,1.7,-0.1285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
  \draw[msky!70!black]
    (0.6,0.3,2.0705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,0.3,0.2705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,1.7,-1.9285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0.6,1.7,-0.1285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
  % the line r(t) = (t + pi/2, 1, -t) for t in [-0.95, 0.82]
  \draw[mred, very thick] (0.6208,1,0.95) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.3908,1,-0.82);
  % the point P0
  \fill[red] (1.5708,1,0) circle (2pt) node[below left=2pt, red] {$P_0\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2},1,0\right)$};
  % the normal vector at P0, direction (1, pi/2, 1)/|.| * 0.95
  \draw[-stealth, mblue, very thick] (1.5708,1,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.0146,1.6971,0.4438)
        node[below right=3pt] {$\vec n=\left\langle 1,\frac{\pi}{2},1\right\rangle$};
  % label for the line
  \node[mred, below right=0pt] at (2.25,1.05,-0.72) {$\mathbf r(t)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-3">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>S</m> be a sphere centered at the origin in <m>\R^3</m>, and
consider any point <m>P</m> on <m>S</m>. Show that the vector
<m>\vec{OP}</m> is orthogonal to the tangent plane to <m>S</m> at
<m>P</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>R</m> be the radius of the sphere, and write
<m>P = (x_0, y_0, z_0)</m>. Assume for the moment that <m>P</m> lies on
the top half of the sphere (i.e. <m>z_0 \gt 0</m>). Note that the top
half of the sphere is the same as the graph of the function
<m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{R^2 - x^2 - y^2}</m>. We can compute
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0, y_0)
\amp = -\frac{x_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x_0, y_0)
\amp = -\frac{y_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}},</mrow>
</md>
so that the tangent plane to the graph at <m>P</m> (which is the same
as the tangent plane to <m>S</m> at <m>P</m>) is given by
<md>
<mrow>
\amp z - z_0
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = -\frac{x_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}}\,(x - x_0)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad {} - \frac{y_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}}\,(y - y_0).
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We can rewrite this as
<md>
<mrow>
\amp x_0(x - x_0) + y_0(y - y_0) + z_0(z - z_0)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = 0,
</mrow>
</md>
where we use the fact that <m>z_0 = \sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}</m> to
simplify things. From this we see that
<m>\langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle</m> is a normal vector to the tangent
plane. But <m>\langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle = \vec{OP}</m>, so this is
exactly what we needed to show. See <xref ref="fig-rev-tp-sphere"/>.
</p>

<p>
A similar argument (with <m>-\sqrt{R^2 - x^2 - y^2}</m> in place of
<m>\sqrt{R^2 - x^2 - y^2}</m>) works when <m>P</m> is on the bottom
half of the sphere (i.e. <m>z_0 \lt 0</m>). When <m>z_0 = 0</m>, we
have to use a <q>sideways graph</q> of some function like
<m>f(y,z) = \sqrt{R^2 - y^2 - z^2}</m>, but other than that, pretty
much everything is the same.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-tp-sphere">
<caption>
The vector <m>\vec{OP} = \langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle</m> from the
center of the sphere to the point <m>P</m> is a normal vector to the
tangent plane to <m>S</m> at <m>P</m>.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
A sphere centered at the origin O, drawn with a dashed equator. A
point P sits on the upper right of the sphere. An arrow from O through
P continues past the surface, and a small shaded parallelogram at P,
perpendicular to the arrow, represents the tangent plane. A right
angle marker at P shows that the vector O P is orthogonal to the
tangent plane.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % the sphere
  \draw[thick, mblue] (0,0) circle (2);
  \draw[mblue, dashed] (-2,0) arc (180:360:2 and 0.55);
  \draw[mblue] (-2,0) arc (180:0:2 and -0.55);
  % center
  \fill (0,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$O$};
  % the point P at 40 degrees
  \coordinate (P) at (40:2);
  % OP vector, extended past the sphere
  \draw[-stealth, mred, very thick] (0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (40:2.9)
        node[above right=-1pt] {$\vec{OP}$};
  \fill[red] (P) circle (2pt) node[below right=2pt] {$P$};
  % tangent plane at P: parallelogram perpendicular to OP
  % direction along the page perpendicular to OP: (-sin40, cos40)
  % slanted second direction to fake 3D
  \begin{scope}[shift={(P)}]
    \coordinate (u) at (-0.766,0.643);   % perpendicular to OP, unit
    \coordinate (v) at (0.30,0.42);      % skewed depth direction
    \fill[morange, opacity=0.30]
      ($-1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$)
      ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($-1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
    \draw[morange!70!black]
      ($-1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$)
      ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($-1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
    % right-angle marker between OP direction and the plane direction u
    \draw ($0.34*(u)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($0.34*(u) + (0.26,0.218)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0.26,0.218);
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-33">
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation of the tangent plane to the sphere
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 14</m> at <m>(1,2,3)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Write the sphere as the level surface
<m>F(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 14</m>. The gradient is normal to the
level surface:
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \nabla F = \langle 2x, 2y, 2z\rangle,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \vec n = \nabla F\big|_{(1,2,3)} = \langle 2, 4, 6\rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
Hence the tangent plane is
<md>
2(x-1) + 4(y-2) + 6(z-3) = 0,
</md>
which simplifies to <m>x + 2y + 3z = 14</m>. (Notice that the normal
vector is parallel to the position vector <m>\langle 1,2,3\rangle</m>,
exactly as we proved for spheres centered at the origin.)
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Tangent Planes and
     Differentials": its problems 9 and 11 ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="pp-40">
<statement>
<p>
You measure the magnitude of a force <m>F</m> to be <m>6</m>, but
there could be an error of at most <m>\pm 0.3</m>. You measure a mass
<m>M</m> to be <m>3</m> with an error of at most <m>\pm 0.1</m>. You
are trying to use these two to approximate the magnitude of the
acceleration <m>A</m>. We have <m>A = \dfrac{F}{M}</m>. Use the
principle of the differential to find an upper bound on the absolute
value of the error when assuming <m>A = 2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Treat <m>A</m> as a function of the two measured quantities,
<m>A(F,M) = \dfrac{F}{M}</m>. Its differential is
<md>
<mrow>
\amp dA = \frac{\partial A}{\partial F}\,dF + \frac{\partial A}{\partial M}\,dM
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac{1}{M}\,dF - \frac{F}{M^2}\,dM.
</mrow>
</md>
At the measured values <m>F = 6</m> and <m>M = 3</m> (where indeed
<m>A = 2</m>), the coefficients are <m>\frac13</m> and
<m>-\frac{6}{9} = -\frac23</m>.
</p>

<p>
Taking absolute values and using the triangle inequality with the
worst-case errors <m>|dF| \le 0.3</m> and <m>|dM| \le 0.1</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\amp |dA| \le \frac{1}{3}|dF| + \frac{2}{3}|dM|
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad \le \frac13(0.3) + \frac23(0.1) = 0.1 + \frac{1}{15}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac16.
</mrow>
</md>
So the error in the value <m>A = 2</m> is at most about
<m>\frac16 \approx 0.167</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q2-2">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>g(x,y) = \sin(xy)</m>, and note that
<m>g\!\left(\pi, \tfrac13\right) = \tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>. Use an
appropriate partial derivative to approximate
<m>g\!\left(\pi, \tfrac13 + \tfrac1{20}\right)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Only <m>y</m> changes, by <m>\Delta y = \tfrac1{20}</m>, so we need
<m>g_y</m>. Treating <m>x</m> as a constant and using the chain rule,
<md>
g_y(x,y) = x\cos(xy) .
</md>
</p>

<p>
At the point <m>\left(\pi, \tfrac13\right)</m> we have
<m>xy = \tfrac{\pi}{3}</m> and <m>\cos\!\left(\tfrac{\pi}{3}\right)
= \tfrac12</m>, so
<md>
g_y\!\left(\pi, \tfrac13\right) = \pi \cdot \frac12 = \frac{\pi}{2} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
Therefore
<md>
<mrow>g\!\left(\pi, \tfrac13 + \tfrac1{20}\right)
  \amp \approx g\!\left(\pi, \tfrac13\right)
       + g_y\!\left(\pi, \tfrac13\right)\cdot \frac{1}{20}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\sqrt3}{2} + \frac{\pi}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{20}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\sqrt3}{2} + \frac{\pi}{40} \approx 0.944 .</mrow>
</md>
The exact value is
<m>\sin\!\left(\tfrac{23\pi}{60}\right) \approx 0.933</m>, so again the
approximation is quite good.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== Moved from Assignment 9 (Nov 2026) ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="q1-2">
<statement>
<p>
Let
<md>
g(x,y) = \frac{\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt[3]{y}} ,
</md>
and note that <m>g(36,8) = 3</m>. Use an appropriate partial derivative
to approximate <m>g(36,6)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We move from <m>(36,8)</m> to <m>(36,6)</m>. Only <m>y</m> changes, and
it changes by
<md>
\Delta y = 6 - 8 = -2 ,
</md>
so the partial derivative we need is <m>g_y</m>. Writing
<m>g(x,y) = x^{1/2} y^{-1/3}</m>,
<md>
g_y(x,y) = -\frac{1}{3}\, x^{1/2} y^{-4/3}
         = -\frac{\sqrt{x}}{3\, y^{4/3}} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
At <m>(36,8)</m> we have <m>\sqrt{36} = 6</m> and
<m>8^{4/3} = \left(8^{1/3}\right)^4 = 2^4 = 16</m>, so
<md>
g_y(36,8) = -\frac{6}{3 \cdot 16} = -\frac{6}{48} = -\frac{1}{8} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
The linear approximation is
<md>
<mrow>g(36,6) \amp \approx g(36,8) + g_y(36,8)\,\Delta y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3 + \left(-\frac{1}{8}\right)(-2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3 + \frac14 = \frac{13}{4} = 3.25 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
As a check, the exact value is
<m>g(36,6) = 6/\sqrt[3]{6} \approx 3.302</m>, so the approximation is
good to about <m>1.6\%</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the Interactive Problem Set (exercises-runestone-samples.ptx):
     its three Chapter 4 problems.  These use dev-schema elements. ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rs-tf-directional">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If every directional derivative <m>D_{\mathbf u}f(P_0)</m> exists at a point
<m>P_0</m>, then <m>f</m> must be continuous at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
False, and the failure is dramatic. Let
<md>
<mrow>
\amp f(x,y) = \frac{x^2 y}{x^4 + y^2}\ \text{ for } (x,y)\ne(0,0),
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp f(0,0) = 0 .
</mrow>
</md>
For a unit vector <m>\mathbf u = \langle a,b\rangle</m> with <m>b \ne 0</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\amp D_{\mathbf u}f(0,0) = \lim_{t\to 0}\frac{f(ta,tb)}{t}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \lim_{t\to 0}\frac{a^2 b}{t^2a^4 + b^2} = \frac{a^2}{b},
</mrow>
</md>
and <m>D_{\mathbf u}f(0,0) = 0</m> when <m>b = 0</m>. So <em>every</em>
directional derivative exists. Yet along the parabola <m>y = mx^2</m>,
<md>
f(x,mx^2) = \frac{m}{1+m^2},
</md>
which depends on <m>m</m> — different parabolas approach different values,
so <m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)}f</m> does not exist and <m>f</m> is not
continuous at the origin. Existence of directional derivatives is a
statement about behavior along <em>straight lines</em> only; continuity
requires control along <em>every</em> approach.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs-clickable-error">
<title>Find the Error</title>
<statement>
<p>
Below is a student's computation of the derivative of
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 y</m> at the point <m>(1,2)</m> in the direction of
<m>\vec v = \langle 3,4\rangle</m>. Exactly one line contains the error.
Click on that line.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Not quite. Check each line against the one before it, and ask which
kind of vector the directional-derivative formula needs. The solution
below explains the error.
</p>
</feedback>
<areas>
<p><area correct="no"><m>\nabla f = \left\langle 2xy,\; x^2\right\rangle</m></area></p>
<p><area correct="no"><m>\nabla f(1,2) = \left\langle 4,\; 1\right\rangle</m></area></p>
<p><area correct="yes"><m>D_{\vec v}f(1,2) = \nabla f(1,2)\cdot\vec v
  = (4)(3) + (1)(4) = 16</m></area></p>
<p><area correct="no">So the directional derivative equals <m>16</m>.</area></p>
</areas>
<solution>
<p>
The error is in the third line. The formula
<m>D_{\mathbf u}f = \nabla f\cdot\mathbf u</m> requires a <em>unit</em>
vector. Since <m>\left|\vec v\right| = 5</m>, the correct direction is
<m>\mathbf u = \left\langle \tfrac35, \tfrac45 \right\rangle</m>, giving
<md>
<mrow>D_{\mathbf u}f(1,2) \amp = 4\cdot\tfrac35 + 1\cdot\tfrac45</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{16}{5} = 3.2,</mrow>
</md>
not <m>16</m>. Lines 1, 2 and 4 are each correct given the line before
them: line 4 faithfully reports the (wrong) number produced by line 3.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>